From 63dc0cff887dd8a4597eb485c23e4ef01b4aee6e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: feixiangpeng <155504520+feixiangpeng@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 21:19:49 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] [Spec] DSPARK: hand the overlap readback a snapshot of
 the folded accept (#42465)

The folded accept writes fixed epilogue buffers inside the target-verify
graph, and the next replay rewrites them. accept_and_finalize returned
read_accept(bs) views, which the DSPARK worker put into
GenerationBatchResult.accept_lens / next_token_ids. The overlap scheduler
reads those back on copy_stream after copy_stream.wait_stream(forward_stream)
and launches the next forward without waiting for the copy, so when the copy
engine is busy (HiCache write-through D2H of a long prefix) the readback runs
after replay N+1 and returns step N+1's accept lengths. Each TP rank races on
its own, so ranks committed different token counts and deadlocked.

Add DsparkVerifyEpilogue.snapshot_accept: clones of the accept buffers made
on the forward stream right after the replay, so stream order pins them to
step N and async_d2h's record_stream keeps them alive for the readback.
accept_and_finalize returns the snapshot on the folded path. read_accept
keeps returning views because the in-graph _accept writes through them
(build_accept_outs(out=self.read_accept(bs))); cloning there, as #42812
does, leaves the static buffers stale on current main.

Tests: a CPU case in test_dspark_accept_tail.py (fails on main, passes with
the fix) and a 1-GPU test that replays the two-stream interleaving
deterministically.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
